  <ym-title|Character Ward inheritance and common continuation
  application><label|character-ward-inheritance-and-common-continuation-application>

  Parent author draft, issue26, session hairer-codex-existence-20261111.
  Pin3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464. Prose:
  manuscript/existence/phase-ward-inheritance.tex. Independent review, LaTeX
  integration/render and structural check pending.

  This fills the explicit phase-flux caller join identified by external
  issue11 review6818544207, and records the mixed/selected continuation
  application. It assumes the actual unselected normalized stress law and the
  already-proved slab/character/null-space interfaces. It does not produce
  shell nodes or free Ward classifiers, nor claim every character has
  UniqueHeat. No new public conclusion beyond the established interfaces is
  inferred.

  Full reads at this pin:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>YM.Work.B09.Core.lean (path Lean/YM/Work/B09/Core.lean):
    InsideTimes, Padded, exists_padded, middle, middle_diff, Evaluates,
    has_evaluator, tester_ext, exterior_not_inside, matrix_of_placed,
    matrix_at_center. Qualified declarations use namespace YM.Work.B09.
    Character extension is only complex bounded linear outside the
    compression algebra. Same evaluator for every fixed translated slab/field
    configuration.

    <item>YM.Work.B09.{Evaluates.inclusions,Evaluates.derivatives},
    Differential.lean: constant slab and translation on an open configuration
    neighborhood; slot derivative through continuous evaluator. Already
    covered by canonical phase-regularity; not re-proved as a separate
    theorem here.

    <item>YM.Work.B09.{window_extends,matrix_on_shell,weight_vanish,weight_within,
    padded_shell,shell_integrable,integrated,pushed,Evaluates.flux},
    Shell.lean: whole support of window lies inside one slab; derivative
    weight vanishes near every collision. Norm-Bochner integration precedes
    evaluation. Extra word spectators outside whole window make unselected
    Ward laws applicable.

    <item>YM.Work.B09.answer, Lean/YM/Work/B09.lean: applies that same
    evaluator for every character and returns inclusion/derivative/flux data.

    <item>YM.OS.Selection.Certificate, Lean/YM/Phase/Mixture.lean: flux is
    for EVERY character, not only the selected/typical phase. Read entire
    file, including whole-word receives, compact-character resolution and
    barycenter.

    <item>YM.Spec.{Seed,Locals,Materializes,Relativistic,Final},
    Spec/Closed.lean: allWorld is unselected contour; chosen is selected
    contour; qt realizes chosen. Read full file; no identification of these
    distinct contours.

    <item>YM.Next.BOUNDARY.{unselected_pantry,selected_pantry,both,after_materializes},
    Next/BOUNDARY/Stage.lean: read full file. Same reconstruction arcs apply
    without UniqueHeat to mixed FieldCore. Selected Compatible.vacuum invokes
    heat uniqueness; unselected does not. Existing full-tube/locality/domain
    arguments supply the mathematical mechanisms, not a new invocation of an
    unproved generic OS reconstruction theorem.

    <item>YM.Pert.withFinal, Pert/Construction.lean: phase Looms then
    after_materializes on same Seed, algebra, selected character and spins.

    <item>YM.EveryPhase.{Completed,all_characters},
    EveryPhase/Extension.lean: read for scope. Its all-character UniqueHeat,
    low-energy, irreducibility, continued Minkowski stress and
    boundary-mixture clauses are stronger than the mixed/selected application
    here; their existence in this source is not claimed as a proof of them in
    the present chapter.
  </itemize-minus>

  Canonical reuse: phases, phase-selection, phase-regularity,
  reflection-transport, phase-fields, slab-regularity;
  physical-angular-actions for normalized stress; complex-heat,
  analytic-boundary, fourier-support, label-descent, lorentz-covariance,
  full-tube, heat-vectors, reconstruction, boundary-detection,
  spectral-measure and locality. These are exact retained interfaces, not
  closure of their analytic construction dependencies. Hahn--Banach and
  Bochner integral commutation are standard functional-analysis background
  already used by the canonical phase chapters; the latter is explained by
  simple approximation. No new literature theorem substitution or search
  claim.

  Commands actually run: scoped rg, cat and sed on named files and canonical
  chapters; whole-manuscript rg for phase/stress/flux wording found no
  existing explicit transfer argument. This search is a navigation aid, not
  an exhaustive negative theorem-coverage certificate. No Lean edits, build
  or axiom replay.

